<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danelaw</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Danelaw was the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> settled and ruled by Scandinavians in the late 9th and 10th centuries, north and east of a line roughly along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a>. It took shape after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> settled Northumbria, East Anglia and eastern Mercia, and its boundary was set out in Alfred's treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a>. West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> kings reconquered it in the 10th century, but Danish law and place names long survived there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/danelaw.jpg" alt="Danelaw" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:England_878.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alfred the Great (r. 871–899) held off the Great Heathen Army and fortified Wessex with a network of burhs, and his son Edward the Elder and daughter Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, reconquered the <b>Danelaw</b> south of the Humber in the 910s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vikings installed Ceolwulf II and in 877 divided the kingdom, settling the eastern half, later part of the <b>Danelaw</b>, and leaving the west to him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Scandinavian settlement in the <b>Danelaw</b>, and the rule of Cnut and other Danish kings in the early 11th century, brought speakers of Old Norse into close contact with English.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Danelaw</b> of eastern and northern England, Norse and English speakers lived side by side, and hundreds of place names ending in -by, “farm” or “village”, and -thorpe, “outlying settlement”, such as Derby, Grimsby, Whitby and Scunthorpe, mark their settlements.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The army’s followers settled the <b>Danelaw</b>, and a Viking kingdom at York lasted until 954.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Each borough was the base of a jarl and his army, controlling the surrounding district, in an area later known as part of the <b>Danelaw</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw">Five Boroughs of the Danelaw</a></li></ul></section>
